#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "1ad81fed-5e71-5df6-abc4-a09cd5c432fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10362" :question/problem "Suppose that in a population of children with only one pet, $42\\%$ of children have a dog. A random sample of $n = 40$ children is taken from the population and asked what pet they have.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}$, the proportion of children in the sample that have a dog? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$, and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic,; 0, otherwise$.\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n$, we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\np = 42 \\% = 0.42\n$$\n\nand $n = 40$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[p^(^)] &= 42% = 0.42, \\\\\nVar[p^(^)] &= (0.42(1 - 0.42))/(40) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.0061\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161704 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/id #uuid "b162ec77-b73f-50f7-b2d2-b6c82bdb7d67" :question/math-academy-id "e-10363" :question/problem "Workers of a small company decided to elect a new union representative. There are $50$ workers, and $60\\%$ of this worker population will vote for candidate $A$. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population will vote for candidateA; 0, otherwise$\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50$. Find $P(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0)$." :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N = 50$, and $p = 0.6$ represents the proportion of the population that will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The number of members that will vote for candidate $A$ equals\n$Np = 50(0.6) = 30$.\n- The number of members that will *not* vote for candidate $A$ equals\n$N(1 - p) = 50(1 - 0.6) = 20$.\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0)\n$$\n\nthe probability that the second member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ given that the first member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ either.\n\nIf the first member does *not* vote for candidate $A$, we have\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nN - 1 = 50 - 1 = 49\n$$\n\nmembers remaining, and\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nN(1 - p)-1 = 19\n$$\n\nmembers remaining that will not vote for candidate $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0) = \\dfrac{N(1 - p)-1}{N-1} = \\dfrac{19}{49}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161705 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "a623014c-203f-5c40-87b5-e847782a8ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10364" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 360$ from a population where $35 \\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nwhere $i = 1, \\ldots, 360$. If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n$\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{360} X_i$\nfind approximate values of $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$, and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n$, the random variables $X_i$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p) \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X$, defined as\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\np = 35 \\% = 0.35, \\qquad n = 360\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(360,0.35)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= np \\\\\n&= 360 \\cdot 0.35 \\\\\n&= 126 \\\\\nVar[X] &= np(1 - p) \\\\\n&= 360 \\cdot 0.35 \\cdot (1 - 0.35) \\\\\n&= 81.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a748cda8-ea9d-5144-8d98-03dcd0282eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159753} {:answer/id #uuid "bc66e4d9-0c3b-5e1b-8243-547eba1e3923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159754} {:answer/id #uuid "33ecdc30-b74b-5b05-829b-acc4aa5bc7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159755} {:answer/id #uuid "617e3e5e-7c6d-5fec-b6c4-3a90708a4237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159756} {:answer/id #uuid "6eafe5c7-8902-5ff7-abe8-f0d1ba5d045a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159757}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e0335b40-727a-5f83-9601-a606a9bb5b72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159752}] :question/id #uuid "2b52b200-3164-5ecb-b7e7-f226f8cd59f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-146167" :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N = 20$, where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$X_{i} = {1, ith member of the population has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor $i = 1, …, 20$. Find $P(X_{2} = 1 \\mid X_{1} = 1)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a950e4e7-15c6-5d67-b822-d815f13a51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[p^(^)] = 0.45, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159789} {:answer/id #uuid "bd56ba8b-0c04-5716-93ab-02934427d9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[p^(^)] = 0.45, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159790} {:answer/id #uuid "c83c6cc7-5f70-5b7a-9a11-c5d579289a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[p^(^)] = 0.45, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159791} {:answer/id #uuid "f148765b-9be7-53c8-a58e-5304ee78e3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[p^(^)] = 0.55, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159792} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1321a2-cd23-5b5f-8748-83d80168c59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[p^(^)] = 0.55, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159793}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ff33034c-efba-5bcc-a6c7-948ce58e66bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159788}] :question/id #uuid "fba7207c-8537-5b1a-b7be-0bdd1b79e13d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146295" :question/problem "Suppose that $45%$ of the voting population plans to vote for candidate $A$ in the upcoming mayoral election. A random sample of size $n = 20$ is taken from the population and asked about their voting preference.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $p^(^)$, the proportion of voters in the sample that plan to vote for candidate $A$? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cb4075-7652-5e31-874e-e3157e2b99a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[X] = 9, Var[X] = 4.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159801} {:answer/id #uuid "ceab0efc-22f6-57d8-aa1c-3673e9ef6ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[X] = 9, Var[X] = 4.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159802} {:answer/id #uuid "ed842e3a-4c84-5d71-be86-af4d9a25f11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[X] = 11, Var[X] = 4.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159803} {:answer/id #uuid "2f29dc05-037c-51ea-9b79-7af39d58b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[X] = 11, Var[X] = 3.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159804} {:answer/id #uuid "37fd8a1a-38f0-5684-8745-468f93236fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E[X] = 11, Var[X] = 4.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159805}] :answer-field/id #uuid "38d82244-a4dd-54d0-9434-f6f13beddc2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159800}] :question/id #uuid "fe2c4a18-2ed9-51a4-8a01-2bc608b90a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-146303" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 20$ from a population where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$X_{i} = {1, ith member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\n\nwhere $i = 1, …, 20$. If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(20)X_{i}$\nfind approximate values of $E[X]$ and $Var[X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population si